          "title": "About UK Prime Minister Transition",
          "subject": "UK Prime Minister Transition",
          "summary": "This event concerns the transfer of leadership of the UK government from Keir Starmer to Andy Burnham after Starmer announced that he would resign as Labour leader and prime minister. In the UK’s parliamentary system, a change of prime minister can occur within an existing Parliament when the Sovereign appoints the person judged most likely to command the confidence of the House of Commons; it does not itself require a general election.",
          "facts": [
            {
              "label": "Transition date",
              "value": "The leadership transition was expected to conclude on 20 July 2026, the end of Labour’s nomination stage; parliamentary material described Burnham as the sole confirmed candidate at the time and said he would become Labour leader and prime minister if unopposed.",
              "source_ids": [
                "o1"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Predecessor and resignation",
              "value": "Keir Starmer, who had been prime minister since 5 July 2024, announced on 22 June 2026 that he would resign as Labour leader.",
              "source_ids": [
                "o2",
                "o1"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Incoming leader",
              "value": "Andy Burnham was elected MP for Makerfield on 18 June 2026 and is recorded by the UK Parliament as Labour Party leader from 17 July 2026.",
              "source_ids": [
                "o3"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Constitutional basis",
              "value": "When a prime minister resigns, the Sovereign invites the person most likely to command House of Commons confidence to serve as prime minister and form a government; the relevant political parties are expected to make clear who that person is.",
              "source_ids": [
                "o4"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Election implications",
              "value": "Replacing a prime minister does not automatically dissolve Parliament or trigger a general election. Under the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022, dissolution is by the Sovereign using prerogative powers, conventionally following a prime ministerial request, and a Parliament otherwise has a maximum five-year term.",
              "source_ids": [
                "o5"
              ]
            }
